
            _testContext.WriteLine("OLD ARRAY LIST COUNT: {0}, NEW ARRAY LIST COUNT: {1}, OLD X: {2}, PRE-INDEX OF X: {3}, POST-INDEX OF X: {4}, LAST INDEX OF X: {5}, OLD ARR LIST CONTAINS X?: {6}, NEW ARR LIST CONTAINS X?: {7}",
                Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, Old_x, Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX, New_Ret, Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_x == x);
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == (Old_arrListContainsX ? New_Ret : -1)); 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == (Old_arrListContainsX ? Old_arrListIndexOfX : -1));
        }
    }
}

Kindly tell me what might be wrong with my test case and how can I fix it?

A: When performing a mutation testing (a type of white-box testing that ensures your program behaves correctly as a whole), the ArrayList's state is not preserved between mutations (the individual tests). 
So when the mutation tests run LastIndexOf, it is using an empty list. 
Hence, the test always succeeds with an incorrect assumption (AssumePrecondition) that you are checking in the test, which always returns -1 when the list is empty.
Try ensuring that the arraylist is having some values before running the test for LastIndexOf.
Also, do not assume under tests, remove that line from the method because you are already assuming in PexAssumeUnderTest attribute,
Another way to deal with this problem, you can use a PexInstrumentationProvider to provide your own logic. 
I would suggest you to add a method that will clear the ArrayList and add some items (with specific values and count) before running LastIndexOf tests.
[PexMethod]
public void InsertItemsForTests()
{
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

    arrList.Add(1);
    arrList.Add(2);
    arrList.Add(3);

    // now your LastIndexOf test should work as expected 
}

With this setup, all your other tests will already have some data in the ArrayList, so LastIndexOf shouldn't have problems when run.
You can find more information about PexInstrumentationProvider in the Pex documentation.
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/people/simonpj/papers/pldi11-preparation.pdf

A: You are right in considering the fact that the order might be different for different testing. In order to work around this and ensure your initialization is working properly and the order of numbers in your list is not a concern, you can add a method to your test assembly that initialize the list before your tests are executed.
Here is an example:
public class InitializeArrListTest
{
    [AssemblyInitialize]
    public static void AssemblyInitialize(TestContext testContext)
    {
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

        arrList.Add(1);
        arrList.Add(2);
        arrList.Add(3);

        testContext.Properties["arrList"] = arrList;
    }
}

Now you can consume this list in your tests like this:
public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    ArrayList testList = (ArrayList)TestContext.Properties["arrList"];
    // rest of your code here
}

This way, regardless of the test run order, you are always working with the same ArrayList instance.
Another approach is to utilize PexInstrumentationProvider to control the initialization phase of your tests.
Another important point to mention that the order of items in the list does not have an impact on the LastIndexOf functionality, so this should not make your test cases fail. Just remember that you'll need to ensure your initialization phase is working properly and that the order of numbers in your list is not your main concern when running your tests.
Hope this helps.
Please let me know if you have any further queries or if there is anything I can help you with.
Thank you.

