A: The problem I found with the contract is that the assertion should not be dependent on the new list state as it is. The new array should have the same state before it started the operation (which is my case the array was modified). Here is the corrected code:
Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
Assert.AreEqual(Old_x, x);

if (Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 && New_Ret == -1)
{
   Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
}
else
{
   Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_Ret);
   Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
}

And now it works perfectly with the PexRunner.
Thank you for your help.

A: Your contract seems to be missing an assertion that checks whether the last index of the item in the ArrayList is always less than or equal to the ArrayList's Count. 
You can ensure this by adding an assertion line like this:
Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret <= New_arrListCount);

This ensures that the returned index is a valid index within the ArrayList and is not out of bounds.
Besides that, when adding items to the ArrayList manually via IndexOf add/remove operations, Pex or any similar tools may fail to explore all possible branches causing it to miss certain potential paths under certain circumstances. In such cases you may need to manually add those paths.
Hope these suggestions help you to get the test coverage going as you intend.

A: Firstly, the method ArrayList.LastIndexOf() is not part of the ArrayList Contract, you should only include methods that really belong to the ArrayList API in contract testing.
Secondly, you need to ensure that LastIndexOf() is tested with values that are truly at the end of the list, not always with the last item in the list. The contract testing tools might not test with values at the end due to various reasons (like path coverage).
Lastly, it's a good idea to make sure that LastIndexOf() is only called with values that exist in arrList, testing with a value that doesn't exist in the list will also help ensure this condition is tested.
Hope these suggestions help you to get your contract tests better.
Regarding the part where the condition for the contract testing is:  If the return value is negative, old array list doesnt contain x; and if the return value is positive, new array list contains x; the condition should change to: if return value is negative, new array list doesn't contain x; and if the return value is positive, new array list contains x., to ensure that the new state (including the state after LastIndexOf()) of the array list is correctly monitored.
Remember you should only add test cases that your contract verifies, and you won't get any points for a contract that passes without the corresponding tests.
Hope these suggestions help you to get your contract tests better.
Regarding the part where the condition for the contract testing is: if return value is negative, new array list doesn't contain x; and if the return value is positive, new array list contains x; the condition should change to: if return value is negative, new array list doesn't contain x; and if the return value is positive, new array list contains x., to ensure that the new state of the array list is correctly monitored.
Remember you should only add test cases that your contract verifies, and you won't get any points for a contract that passes without the corresponding tests.
Hope these suggestions help you to get your contract tests better.

