Also as a note, Pex cannot guarantee to test edge cases, just the most common cases. If you want to test the edge cases you have to specify the edge cases using PexAssume and then test the cases within each edge.

A: ArrayList.LastIndexOf(Object) returns an integer indicating the last position of the object in the ArrayList. If it cannot find object then return -1. The complexity of LastIndexOf method in ArrayList is O(n) because it just traverse the whole list to find the last index of specified object.
As a part of the contract testing, you are trying to prove that LastIndexOf method should not modify the state of list (it should be a pure function). So the only change that you are expecting from the LastIndexOf method is the value that it returned as result and not the state of the list itself. 
LastIndexOf returns -1 when the specified object is not found in the ArrayList. So there is no need to check the count or the contains method's result. Also, LastIndexOf should never increase or decrease the count of the list.
So the properties you are testing are:
- LastIndexOf method should return index if element is present else -1.
- LastIndexOf method should not modify the state of list.
Also since LastIndexOf is not tested for edge cases like when ArrayList is empty. So you have to test these scenarios as well.
Here is a sample Pex-generated contract test:

public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    //Assuming ArrayList arrList contains element x.
    Assume.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

    //Execution of LastIndexOf method.
    int actualResult = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    //Check that returned index is the last occurrence of x in arrList.
    Assert.IsTrue(Enumerable.Range(0, arrList.Count).Last(i => arrList[i] == x) == actualResult);

    //Ensure that arrList remains unchanged.
    Assert.AreEqual(arrList, arrList);
}

The first assertion checks if the LastIndexOf method returns a valid index (the index of the last occurrence of x) and the second assertion checks that the list state remains unchanged after LastIndexOf operation.

A: LastIndexOf is a standard function of ArrayList, if a value does not exist in the ArrayList, it returns -1. The point of your method is to prove that LastIndexOf will not modify anything to the ArrayList, just return the last index of the element or -1. The key word here is the "point". 

Here is how you can implement your test:

public void TestLastIndexOf([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    //Precondition Assume: x exists in arrList
    Assume.That(arrList.Contains(x));
         
    //Test
    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
    int oldIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int result = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
           
    //Postcondition Assert
    //LastIndexOf should return the Last index of element X in the list.
    Assert.AreEqual(oldIndexOfX, result);
         
    //LastIndexOf should not modify the list's Count property.
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount, arrList.Count);           
}

This test assumes an element x exists in the list before--------------------